te_neutral":0.5294,"def_epa_per_play":0.4984},{"week":16,"opponent":"LAC","home":false,"pf":16.0,"pa":19.0,"off_snaps":76,"epa_per_play":0.01998,"pass_rate":0.6316,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4167,"def_epa_per_play":-0.02154},{"week":17,"opponent":"LV","home":true,"pf":31.0,"pa":32.0,"off_snaps":76,"epa_per_play":-0.003464,"pass_rate":0.5789,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5942,"def_epa_per_play":0.06659}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2024,"team":"TEN","distance":0.1572,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"CAR","distance":0.3193,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2018,"team":"ARI","distance":0.3763,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"PIT","distance":0.4252,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"CHI","distance":0.4702,"shared":18,"play_caller":null}],"defense":[{"season":2021,"team":"LAC","distance":0.7886,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2023,"team":"LA","distance":0.8153,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Raheem Morris"},{"season":2018,"team":"NYG","distance":0.8467,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"BAL","distance":0.8747,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Mike Macdonald"},{"season":2023,"team":"MIA","distance":0.9076,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Vic Fangio"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Broncos went 5-11 in 2020 and were outscored 446 to 323. The defense did most of the work, finishing 18th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 31st in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the 3rd lowest empty formation usage and the 2nd lowest two-high safety rate in the league.","offense":"The Broncos offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 3rd lowest empty formation usage in the league. Drew Lock ran the passing game, with Jerry Jeudy leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Broncos defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was sack rate, where it ranked 10th, and its weakest was takeaways per game, where it ranked 29th. Shelby Harris was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Malik Reed, who had 8 sacks, after doing little as a pass rusher a year ago. In terms of scheme, they had the 2nd lowest two-high safety rate in the league.","special":"The Broncos were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 10% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 80% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 23% of the money on the books. Their defensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Drew Lock played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,933 yards. Targets were spread around, with Jerry Jeudy and Noah Fant leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Melvin Gordon handled most of the work in the backfield with 56% of the carries. On defense, Malik Reed led the team with 8 sacks and Justin Simmons picked off 5 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Titans and the 2025 Panthers under Dave Canales. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Chargers under Brandon Staley and the 2023 Rams under Raheem Morris.","trends":"The offense went from 24th in EPA per play in 2019 to 31st in 2020, a modest decline. The defense moved from 16th in EPA allowed per play in 2019 to 18th in 2020, about where it was. The biggest change in approach was empty formation usage, which went from 9% to 4%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2020. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}